---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import TryItNow from '../../components/TryItNow.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/CopyPrompt.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Tokenomics"
  description="How Claude usage is priced, how to pick the right model, and the habits that keep costs sane while getting better results."
  pageId="tokenomics"
  readMinutes={5}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="🪙"
    title="Tokenomics"
    subtitle="Great results without burning money. Here's what you actually need to know."
    readMinutes={5}
  />

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      Claude charges per <strong>token</strong> (roughly ¾ of a word). Every message in and out costs tokens.
      Pick the right-sized model for the job, keep conversations focused, and you'll get
      <em>better</em> answers for less money. Win-win.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <!-- What is a token -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>What even is a token?</h2>
    <p>
      Think of tokens as chunks of text — roughly ¾ of a word each. "Aerodynamics" is one token.
      "Hey Claude, can you help me with this?" is about ten. Claude reads your whole message
      (tokens in) and writes a reply (tokens out). Both sides of that exchange get tallied.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not a reason to avoid using Claude. It's a reason to use it <strong>smartly</strong>.
      A focused 50-token ask that gets the right answer on the first try beats a rambling
      500-token saga that needs three rounds of back-and-forth.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Model picker -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>Pick the right model</h2>
    <p>
      Anthropic makes four models of Claude. Same brain, different horsepower — and different price tags.
      Reach for the right one and you'll finish faster <em>and</em> spend less.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- model cards -->
  <div class="my-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <!-- Haiku -->
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
      <div class="mb-3 text-2xl">⚡</div>
      <h3 class="m-0 mb-1 text-lg font-bold text-gold">Haiku</h3>
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">Fast & cheap</p>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text">
        <strong class="text-text">Reach for it when…</strong> you need a quick rewrite,
        a short answer, a yes/no check, or you're doing something repetitive dozens of times.
        Haiku is the espresso shot — small, fast, gets the job done.
      </p>
    </div>
    <!-- Sonnet -->
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border-2 border-gold/60 bg-gold/5 p-5">
      <div class="mb-3 text-2xl">🚗</div>
      <h3 class="m-0 mb-1 text-lg font-bold text-gold">Sonnet</h3>
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">The daily driver · best balance</p>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text">
        <strong class="text-text">Reach for it when…</strong> you're doing real work —
        summarizing a meeting, drafting an email, analyzing a doc, building something in
        Co-Work mode. 90% of your Claude time belongs here.
      </p>
    </div>
    <!-- Opus -->
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
      <div class="mb-3 text-2xl">🏋️</div>
      <h3 class="m-0 mb-1 text-lg font-bold text-text">Opus</h3>
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">The heavyweight</p>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text">
        <strong class="text-text">Reach for it when…</strong> the problem is genuinely hard:
        deep financial analysis, complex multi-step reasoning, evaluating tradeoffs with lots
        of context. Bring out the big gun when the job demands it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <!-- Fable -->
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
      <div class="mb-3 text-2xl">🧙</div>
      <h3 class="m-0 mb-1 text-lg font-bold text-text">Fable</h3>
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm font-medium text-muted">The frontier · premium</p>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text">
        <strong class="text-text">Reach for it when…</strong> the job is huge and multi-step —
        especially when you want one model to <strong>orchestrate</strong> a team of cheaper ones.
        Powerful and pricey; overkill for everyday tasks. More in
        <a href={href('/models/')}>Choosing Your Model</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Cheat-sheet table -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h3>Quick cheat sheet: task → model</h3>
  </div>
  <div class="my-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-[12px] border border-border">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-border bg-card">
          <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-text">Task</th>
          <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-text">Role at Aptera</th>
          <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-gold">Model</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-border">
        <tr class="hover:bg-card-hover">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Quick email rewrite</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">Anyone</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-gold">Haiku ⚡</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hover:bg-card-hover">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Summarize a meeting recording</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">Ops / any team lead</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-gold">Sonnet 🚗</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hover:bg-card-hover">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Draft a supplier RFQ</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">Procurement / Supply Chain</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-gold">Sonnet 🚗</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hover:bg-card-hover">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Complex variance analysis</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">Finance</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-gold">Opus 🏋️</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hover:bg-card-hover">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Translate a short label / instruction</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">Manufacturing / Assembly</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-gold">Haiku ⚡</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hover:bg-card-hover">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Write a Jira epic with acceptance criteria</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">Engineering / PM</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-gold">Sonnet 🚗</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hover:bg-card-hover">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Deep research on a regulation or standard</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">Legal / Compliance</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-gold">Opus 🏋️</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="hover:bg-card-hover">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-text">Batch-format 50 part descriptions</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted">Supply Chain / Eng</td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-gold">Haiku ⚡</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <!-- Habits -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>Habits that save money (and get better answers)</h2>
    <p>
      The biggest lever isn't model choice — it's how you structure your conversations.
      A few small habits compound into dramatically better results.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Start a fresh chat when you change topics.</strong> Every message in a
        conversation gets re-read from scratch on every turn. A 40-message thread about
        solar panel specs is expensive context to drag into a totally different HR question.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Be specific up front.</strong> "Write a three-sentence summary of this
        status update for the exec team" costs one round-trip. "Summarize this" usually costs
        three — one attempt plus two rounds of "make it shorter / more formal / less jargon."
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Don't paste the whole PDF.</strong> You don't need to. Paste the one section
        that's relevant. Claude reads everything you give it every single time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Batch related questions.</strong> If you have five questions about the same
        document, ask all five at once. Five separate chats = five times the overhead.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="Context windows, simply">
    <p>
      Every turn re-reads the whole conversation from the top, so long, unfocused chats cost
      more and can start to drift. Want the full picture — how the window fills and when to
      compact? See the
      <a href={href('/context-window/')} class="font-semibold underline underline-offset-2">Context Window</a>
      lesson.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <TryItNow title="See the difference yourself">
    <p>
      Paste the <strong>focused prompt</strong> into Claude, note the answer. Then paste the
      <strong>rambling one</strong>. Which got there faster?
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 font-semibold text-chat">The focused ask:</p>
    <CopyPrompt text="Rewrite this sentence to be more confident and under 20 words: 'We are hoping to potentially explore the possibility of launching a pilot program sometime in Q3 if conditions allow.'" />
    <p class="mt-4 font-semibold text-muted">The rambling version (costs more, takes longer):</p>
    <CopyPrompt text="Hey so I have this sentence in a doc and I'm not totally sure if it's the right vibe, like it feels a bit wishy-washy? Can you maybe look at it and tell me what you think and potentially rewrite it? Here it is: 'We are hoping to potentially explore the possibility of launching a pilot program sometime in Q3 if conditions allow.' I think it needs to sound more confident but I'm not sure, what do you think is the best approach here?" />
  </TryItNow>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>Tokens are the currency — every word in and out counts. Use Claude smartly, not sparingly.</li>
      <li>Match the model to the job: Haiku for quick tasks, Sonnet for daily work, Opus for heavy analysis, Fable for the biggest multi-step projects.</li>
      <li>Fresh chats are cheaper <em>and</em> better — don't drag stale context into a new topic.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>
</BaseLayout>
